      textHtml: `<h3>Trick-or-treat!</h3><p>Many different cultures have had traditions about the dead. People in places like Ireland, China, Egypt and Mexico believed that souls needed food. They thought the food <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="питала">nourished</span> them on their journey from <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="кладбищ">cemeteries</span> to heaven. People had to put out good things for souls to eat. However, if the food <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="гнила">rotted</span> or <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="портилась">spoiled</span>, the soul got mad. The <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="злая, зловещая">wicked</span> soul might <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="проклясть">curse</span> the family and make them <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="голодать">starve</span> during the winter.</p><p>In other places, people begged for food on a holiday that remembers the souls of dead saints. People wore <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="маскировку, маскарадные костюмы">disguises</span> with <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="капюшонами">hoods</span> that covered their faces. If they did not get food, they played a trick on the home. For this reason, the activity is known as “trick-or-treating.” <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="Вскоре">Shortly</span> after people first began trick-or-treating, parents started sending their children to beg on that day. Housewives gave the children food if they performed a song or a dance. When people moved to America from all over the world, they brought this tradition with them. <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="Жители">Inhabitants</span> of villages started trick-or-treating in the early 1900s. In 1939 a children’s <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="издание, публикация">publication</span> <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="познакомила, ознакомила">acquainted</span> the whole country with the tradition. It became very popular.</p><p>Today, trick-or-treaters are not begging for food, and they are not <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="напуганы">scared</span> of souls. They just enjoy the <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="возбуждение, острые ощущения">thrill</span> of dressing up like creatures and getting candy. Ghosts and <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="скелеты">skeletons</span> are favorite costumes. But some children wear <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="нарядные, необычные">fancy</span> disguises, like <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="пираты">pirates</span>. They carry <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="фонарики">flashlights</span> instead of fires. In some places, children still perform songs or <span class="keyword-tooltip" data-tooltip="загадки">riddles</span> to get candy. But most of the time, they just say “Trick-or-Treat!”</p>`,
      videoUrl: "https://www.youtube.com/embed/5jPySNrCFm0",
      keywordsList: [{ word: "acquaint", translation: "знакомить, ознакомлять" }, { word: "cemetery", translation: "кладбище" }, { word: "curse", translation: "проклинать" }, { word: "disguise", translation: "маскировка, маскарадный костюм" }, { word: "fancy", translation: "нарядный, изысканный" }, { word: "flashlight", translation: "карманный фонарик" }, { word: "hood", translation: "капюшон" }, { word: "inhabitant", translation: "житель, обитатель" }, { word: "nourish", translation: "питать, кормить" }, { word: "pirate", translation: "пират" }, { word: "publication", translation: "публикация, издание" }, { word: "riddle", translation: "загадка" }, { word: "rot", translation: "гнить, разлагаться" }, { word: "scare", translation: "пугать, отпугивать" }, { word: "shortly", translation: "вскоре, в скором времени" }, { word: "skeleton", translation: "скелет" }, { word: "spoil", translation: "портиться, портить" }, { word: "starve", translation: "голодать, умирать с голоду" }, { word: "thrill", translation: "трепет, острые ощущения" }, { word: "wicked", translation: "злой, нечестивый" }],
      testUrl: "https://slike7236-hash.github.io/universal-quiz-server/?lesson=ess3_5",
      games: { matchup: "...", anagrama: "...", typeanswer: "...", wordsearch: "...", pair: "..." }
